# Boards: machines whose memory layout no loader or firmware reports.
#
# An x86 PC's firmware hands over E820 or a UEFI map, and aarch64 and riscv32 boards
# hand over a device tree. A Cortex-M part has nothing: the kernel is the first code
# on the machine, started from the vector table at reset. What would have been the
# loader's memory map is instead a fact about the board, written down once here and
# emitted by kbuild into the generated configuration as a constant, which
# `boot/info-board` turns into the same memory map the other `bootinfo` providers
# return. See docs/bootloader.md, "Build-time BootInfo".
#
# Each board is a file under boards/ that declares its symbol; BOARD_MEMORY below
# says what that board's memory is. A configuration with no board leaves it empty,
# and nothing reads it.

source "boards/mps2-an385.kcfg"

config BOARD_DESCRIBED
    bool
    readonly
    default y if BOARD_MPS2_AN385
    help
        The configuration's board has a description, so `boot/info-board`
        provides the memory map. Every board file adds its symbol here.

config BOARD_MEMORY
    string
    readonly
    default "kernel-image 0x00000000 0x00400000; usable 0x20000000 0x00400000; usable 0x21000000 0x01000000" if BOARD_MPS2_AN385
    default ""
    help
        The board's physical memory, as `kind start length` entries separated
        by semicolons. Kinds are those of the boot protocol's memory map:
        usable, reserved, kernel-image, boot-data, bad. Parsed at compile time
        by boot/info-board, so a malformed description is a build error, not
        a boot that trusts a wrong map.
